; i2c_pins.pe — I2C pins on a bus that has no I2C hardware.
;
; THERE IS NO I2C CONTROLLER. What the SoC provides is a register file that
; makes each pin's direction a RUNTIME value, and a 1 us tick. Everything an
; I2C bus is — START, STOP, bit cells, arbitration, clock stretching — is
; built from those below.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PINS
;
;   bit 4 = SDA   bit 5 = SCL     both on the SoC's port, both open-drain
;
; Open-drain is the whole reason this milestone exists. A pin is either DRIVEN
; LOW or RELEASED; it is never driven high. On a real bus the external pull-up
; supplies the high level, and a second device may pull the line down at the
; same time — which is not a fault, it is arbitration.
;
; TWO WAYS TO SEND A 1, AND WHY ONLY ONE IS SAFE
;
;   (a) push-pull:  out=1, oe=1    <- drives the pin HIGH. Contention if any
;                                      other device pulls low. Destructive.
;   (b) open-drain: od=1, out=1    <- the OD gate RELEASES the pin instead of
;                                      driving it. Pull-up wins. Safe.
;
; This program uses (b) everywhere. The OD bit makes (a) unreachable: with
; od=1 and out=1 the hardware CANNOT drive high, so the hazardous state is not
; expressible — see rtl/pe_pinmux.v.
;
; But note the idiom, because it is the project thesis in one line:
;
;   to send 1 or 0, write out=1 or out=0, and never touch oe or od again.
;
; The SAME instruction sequence drives a push-pull bus and an open-drain one.
; Only the od register differs, and it is set once. That is what "swap the
; program and it speaks I2C, with the gates unchanged" means concretely.
;
; THIS PROGRAM CLAIMS EXACTLY TWO PINS. PINOE is written directly with 0x30,
; which RELEASES the pins the reset default had enabled (TX, and bits 1-2).
; That is right for an I2C-only program and is the point of a runtime matrix:
; each program owns the pins it uses. A program MIXING protocols must instead
; read-modify-write PINOE — IN A, PINOE / OR A, SDA|SCL / OUT PINOE, A — the
; same idiom spi_xfer.pe uses on the port. Direct writes here are a deliberate
; claim of the whole port, not an oversight.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS PROGRAM IS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT
;
; It is ONE COMPLETE BIT CELL plus a START and a STOP — the pin-level grammar
; of I2C, with the timing actually measured on the wire. It is NOT a byte
; transfer: there is no shift register, no ACK bit, no address phase. Those are
; the transaction layer (plan step 6), and they are built from exactly these
; primitives. A byte transfer is this bit cell wrapped in an 8-iteration loop
; with the ACK slot after it.
;
; Writing the bit cell as one straight-line pass rather than a loop is also
; what makes the timing assertable: a testbench can measure tLOW, tHIGH and the
; period on three edges it can name, instead of measuring a loop.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TIMING, AND WHY THE DELAY IDIOM HAD TO CHANGE
;
; I2CTICK (port 4) increments once per microsecond (60 clocks exactly, so this
; divider has no rounding error at all). Standard-mode floors are tLOW >= 4.7 us,
; tHIGH >= 4.0 us, tBUF >= 4.7 us, with a 100 kHz ceiling (period >= 10 us).
;
; uart_echo.pe and spi_xfer.pe wait a tick with:
;
;     IN A, TICK ; MOV X, A
; w:  IN A, TICK ; SUB A, X ; JZ w
;
; which waits for the counter to CHANGE. The counter is free-running, so that
; delivers somewhere in (0, 1] ticks — up to a full tick short. Harmless for
; SPI (synchronous: the slave times off our own edges, so jitter costs
; nothing) and merely a margin cost for the UART. IT IS NOT SAFE HERE, and the
; first draft of this file proved it: 1,384 cycles for 28 us of nominal delays,
; a 0.83x shortfall.
;
; So this program waits for an exact TARGET instead:
;
;     IN A, I2CTICK ; ADD A, T_LOW ; MOV X, A   ; X = the tick we wait for
; w:  IN A, I2CTICK ; SUB A, X ; JNZ w          ; spin until the counter IS X
;
; SUB's only second operand is an immediate or X — Y is not addressable there —
; so the target lives in X and Y stays free.
;
; THE PHASE RESIDUAL, AND WHY IT SETS THE CONSTANTS. Firmware cannot see where
; in the 60-cycle window a read lands; call that unknown offset phi, 0 <= phi
; < 60 cycles. The target tick arrives 60*N - phi cycles later, so an "N-tick"
; wait delivers (N-1, N] microseconds: STILL UP TO A FULL TICK SHORT. That is
; unavoidable without aligning to a tick boundary first, and the alignment
; costs four more instructions per delay for no gain here.
;
; What it means for the constants is the part that matters: THE NUMBER THAT HAS
; TO CLEAR THE FLOOR IS N-1, NOT N. The counts in T_LOW/T_HIGH/T_STA/T_STO/
; T_BUF (see tools/peasm.py) are chosen so that N-1 clears the floor with
; margin — e.g. tLOW uses 6 ticks, whose worst case is 5 us against a 4.7 us
; floor. A nominal-5 us choice would look compliant and be violating half the
; time, which is exactly the failure a bench test does not catch.
;
; Every instruction between two edges spends part of the window, so the
; measured period is a little LONGER than tLOW + tHIGH. Long is safe here: the
; binding constraint is the 100 kHz ceiling, and the margin to it is what the
; testbench measures. See the measured table in wiki/plans/through-i2c.md.
;
; The 8-bit wrap is safe because the test is EQUALITY, not magnitude: starting
; at tick 254 with N=6 the target wraps to 4, and the counter does reach 4
; (254, 255, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4). The loop also cannot step over the target, because
; it costs 3 cycles against a 60-cycle tick.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE STOP SEQUENCE, WHICH IS NOT THE MIRROR OF START AND IS EASY TO GET WRONG
;
; A STOP is SDA RISING while SCL is HIGH. The trap: if SDA is low and you simply
; release it with SCL already high, you get a STOP — but the way you got SDA low
; matters. Pulling SDA low while SCL is high is a START, so the SDA falling edge
; must happen while SCL is LOW. The first draft of this file drove SDA low while
; SCL was still high and so emitted a spurious second START before the STOP; the
; wire trace showed it (SDA falling with SCL high at cycle 905).
;
; The order that cannot go wrong:
;
;   1. SCL low            (SDA may be anything — no condition is defined with
;                          SCL low)
;   2. SDA low            (safe now: SCL is low)
;   3. tSU;STO wait       (SDA set up before SCL rises)
;   4. release SCL        (SCL rises; SDA still low)
;   5. tHIGH wait         (the SCL high period before the STOP)
;   6. release SDA        (SDA rises while SCL is high — THIS is the STOP)
;   7. tBUF wait          (bus free before the next transaction)
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THE TESTBENCH ASSERTS (dmem layout — the program's observable output)
;
;   dmem[0]  the level of SDA sampled while RELEASED and SCL HIGH — the
;            arbitration sample point, since I2C masters sample data while SCL
;            is high
;   dmem[1]  1 if that read-back was LOW while we were sending a 1 — another
;            master won the bus
;   dmem[2]  the level of SCL read back after releasing it (stretch check)
;   dmem[3]  count of completed START/STOP pairs
;   dmem[4]  the pin byte last driven (so the TB can cross-check TXPIN)

        ; ---- enter open-drain mode, in the order that cannot glitch ---------
        ; OD FIRST, and this is the ONLY place od is written. After it, the
        ; bit-banging below is identical to what a push-pull protocol would run.
        LDI   A, SDA|SCL           ; 0x30
        OUT   PINOD, A

        ; THEN the output levels, BEFORE enabling the pins.
        ;
        ; The order here is not cosmetic. At reset the output register holds
        ; 0x01, so bits 4 and 5 are ZERO. If PINOE were written first, those
        ; two pins would be driven LOW for the two cycles before TXPIN sets
        ; them to 1 -- and on a real bus, SDA falling while SCL is high IS a
        ; START condition. The first version of this file did exactly that and
        ; the wire trace showed the glitch (both lines low at cycles 4-5,
        ; before anything legitimate had happened).
        ;
        ; The OD bit prevents CONTENTION -- it makes "drive high into someone
        ; else's low" inexpressible. It does not prevent driving LOW at the
        ; wrong moment, and a spurious START is exactly that. Setting the level
        ; first means the pins are never enabled while holding the wrong value:
        ; with od=1, out=1 is a RELEASE, so enabling them after writing 0x30
        ; connects them to the pull-ups rather than to a driver.
        LDI   A, SDA|SCL           ; out=1 on both => will read as released
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; THEN claim the two pins (releasing TX and bits 1-2, which the reset
        ; default had enabled -- see the header note on claiming the port).
        LDI   A, SDA|SCL           ; 0x30
        OUT   PINOE, A

        ; ---- clear the result bytes -------------------------------------
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A
        STM   1, A
        STM   2, A
        STM   3, A
        STM   4, A

        ; =================================================================
        ; START — SDA falls while SCL is HIGH. That is the only definition of
        ; a START condition; the same edge with SCL low is just data.
        ; =================================================================
        ; The bus is idle-high here (both released), which is where a START
        ; begins. Only SDA is touched: SCL stays released.
        LDI   A, SCL               ; 0x20 — SCL released, SDA pulled LOW
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; --- tHD;STA >= 4.0 us : hold SDA low before pulling SCL down -----
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        ADD   A, T_STA             ; 6 ticks
        MOV   X, A
w_sta:  IN    A, I2CTICK
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   w_sta

        ; SCL low now that the START is safely behind us: both pins low.
        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; =================================================================
        ; BIT CELL — send a 1 by RELEASING SDA, then sample it.
        ;
        ; The data line changes while SCL is LOW and is valid while SCL is
        ; HIGH. So: release SDA (it rises), hold the low period out, release
        ; SCL (it rises), wait the high period, and SAMPLE WHILE SCL IS HIGH.
        ; That sample point is not decoration — it is exactly when an I2C
        ; master reads the data line, and it is therefore also when
        ; arbitration is decided.
        ; =================================================================
        LDI   A, SDA               ; 0x10 — SDA released (rises), SCL still low
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; --- tLOW >= 4.7 us : the low period of this bit cell ------------
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        ADD   A, T_LOW             ; 6 ticks
        MOV   X, A
w_low:  IN    A, I2CTICK
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   w_low

        ; --- release SCL: it rises, SDA holding 1 ------------------------
        LDI   A, SDA|SCL           ; both released
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; Read SCL back immediately and record it. On a real bus a slave may be
        ; holding SCL low (clock stretching) and the rise does not happen; this
        ; single IN is how a master notices, and it is free with a read-back
        ; path. In a zero-delay simulation the release is instant, so this reads
        ; 1; against a real RC it reads whatever the line has reached.
        IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, SCL
        STM   2, A

        ; --- tHIGH >= 4.0 us : the high period of this bit cell ----------
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        ADD   A, T_HIGH            ; 7 ticks
        MOV   X, A
w_hi:   IN    A, I2CTICK
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   w_hi

        ; --- sample SDA while SCL is HIGH --------------------------------
        ; A released SDA reads the PAD, not our written level — so this read
        ; IS the arbitration comparison. Note it is a single IN: the matrix
        ; makes arbitration visible for free, with no extra status bit and no
        ; comparator. If another master had pulled SDA low, the OD property
        ; means neither of us is driving it, so nothing is damaged — we simply
        ; see a 0 and know to back off.
        IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, SDA
        STM   0, A                 ; record the level we actually saw
        JNZ   arb_ok               ; still high => nobody contended
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   1, A                 ; dmem[1] = we lost arbitration
arb_ok:

        ; --- close the bit cell: SCL back low ----------------------------
        LDI   A, SDA               ; SCL low, SDA released (data still valid)
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; =================================================================
        ; STOP — SDA rises while SCL is HIGH. See the sequence note in the
        ; header; the order below is the one that cannot emit a spurious START.
        ; =================================================================
        ; 1+2. SCL is already low here, so SDA may now be pulled low safely.
        LDI   A, 0x00              ; both pulled low
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; 3. tSU;STO >= 4.0 us — SDA set up before SCL rises
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        ADD   A, T_STO             ; 6 ticks
        MOV   X, A
w_sto:  IN    A, I2CTICK
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   w_sto

        ; 4. release SCL: it rises while SDA is still low
        LDI   A, SCL               ; SCL released, SDA held low
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; 5. tHIGH — the SCL high period ahead of the STOP edge
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        ADD   A, T_HIGH            ; 7 ticks
        MOV   X, A
w_sth:  IN    A, I2CTICK
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   w_sth

        ; 6. release SDA with SCL HIGH -> that rising edge IS the STOP
        LDI   A, SDA|SCL           ; both released, bus goes idle-high
        OUT   TXPIN, A

        ; 7. tBUF >= 4.7 us — bus free time before the next transaction
        IN    A, I2CTICK
        ADD   A, T_BUF             ; 6 ticks
        MOV   X, A
w_buf:  IN    A, I2CTICK
        SUB   A, X
        JNZ   w_buf

        ; =================================================================
        ; DONE — record the completed pair and park the bus idle-high.
        ; =================================================================
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   3, A                 ; dmem[3] = one START/STOP pair completed
        LDI   A, SDA|SCL           ; idle-high, both released
        OUT   TXPIN, A
        STM   4, A                 ; dmem[4] = the byte we are driving
halt:
        JMP   halt                 ; the ISA has no HALT; the TB watches dmem[3]
